<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Four weeks of right-sided throat pain and painful swallowing with a persistent lesion of the right tonsillar pillar in a 62-year-old heavy cigar smoker, suspicious for oropharyngeal cancer. Please examine with laryngoscopy, obtain a biopsy with p16 testing, and advise on staging and next steps. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two weeks of presentation; a contrast CT of the neck is booked for next week so the images are ready for your visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is the right tonsillar lesion a cancer? Please obtain tissue, with p16 testing.</item><item>Does laryngoscopy show any other lesion of the larynx or pharynx?</item><item>What staging is needed beyond the neck CT? I plan a PET/CT once carcinoma is confirmed.</item><item>Who should plan treatment, and what should primary care arrange before it begins: dental care, nutrition, swallowing assessment, and tobacco cessation?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Grant is seen urgently at the request of Dr. Batiste for five weeks of right-sided throat pain and painful swallowing. The pain is 5 of 10 on liquid acetaminophen and the lidocaine rinse. He manages soft foods and liquids without coughing or choking and has lost about 3 kg in all. He has no voice change, ear pain, breathlessness, or blood in his saliva. He smoked about ten cigars a day for more than forty years, inhaling some of the smoke, and stopped a week ago with varenicline; he drinks one or two drinks a week. He wants a diagnosis and a plan quickly so that he can keep working on his memoir. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Hiram Ulysses Grant was born on April 27, 1822, in Point Pleasant, Ohio, the first child of Jesse and Hannah Grant. [D]</item><item>Grant was a heavy cigar smoker: his tobacco use rose during the Civil War to about eighteen to twenty cigars a day, and he reported smoking nine or ten a day in peacetime. [D]</item><item>In June 1884, at his summer home in Long Branch, New Jersey, Grant began to have stinging throat pain and pain on swallowing. [D]</item><item>Grant had recurrent severe, incapacitating headaches, interpreted retrospectively as most likely migraines. [R]</item><item>In 1858 Grant had fever and chills, possibly from malaria or typhoid, and he had periodic fevers believed to have been malaria. [R]</item><item>Grant&apos;s father, Jesse Grant, died in 1873 at age 79; his mother, Hannah Grant, died in 1883. [D]</item></list><paragraph>7 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

