<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Squamous cell carcinoma of the right tonsillar pillar (C09.1). The frozen section confirms squamous cell carcinoma, and with one enlarged right level II node on CT the disease is at least regional. No second lesion is seen in the larynx or pharynx. The stage cannot be assigned until the p16 status is known, because p16-positive and p16-negative oropharyngeal cancers are staged differently, and until PET/CT has looked for distant spread. His long, heavy tobacco use makes p16-negative disease more likely. [S]</item><item>Oropharyngeal dysphagia from the tumor (R13.12). Limited by pain rather than by aspiration: he manages soft foods and liquids without coughing. A swallowing baseline is needed before treatment, which will make swallowing harder for a time. [S]</item><item>Unintended weight loss (R63.4). About 3 kg so far. Nutrition support should start now, before treatment adds to the loss. [S]</item><item>Nicotine dependence, cigars (F17.290). One week abstinent. Staying off tobacco improves the results of treatment and healing and lowers the risk of a second cancer. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Last week&apos;s contrast CT of the neck, showing a right tonsillar mass with one enlarged right level II lymph node; the intake blood count, metabolic panel, albumin, and TSH, all normal; and today&apos;s frozen section, which shows squamous cell carcinoma, with p16 staining and the final report to follow within a few days. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Final pathology with p16 immunohistochemistry within a few days; the result will be telephoned to him and to Dr. Batiste. [S]</item><item>PET/CT for staging next week, as already ordered. [S]</item><item>Referral to the multidisciplinary head and neck oncology team as soon as the final pathology is reported. I will present him there with the PET/CT; for this site the options are transoral surgery with neck dissection or definitive chemoradiation, and the team will advise on the choice. [S]</item><item>Dental evaluation, with fluoride care, before any radiation to the neck. [S]</item><item>Speech-language pathology swallowing baseline before treatment. [S]</item><item>Dietitian assessment now, with a soft, calorie-dense diet and supplement drinks. [S]</item><item>Stay off all tobacco; continue varenicline and counseling with Dr. Batiste. Avoid alcohol. [S]</item><item>Continue liquid acetaminophen, no more than 3,000 mg a day, and the lidocaine rinse, waiting 60 minutes after the rinse before eating or drinking. [S]</item><item>A little blood-streaked saliva over the next day is expected; call or go to the emergency department for bleeding that does not stop. [S]</item><item>The examination and the frozen-section result were discussed with Mr. Grant today; he understands that this is a cancer and that the stage and treatment plan depend on the final report and the PET/CT. Discussed with Dr. Batiste by telephone; Dr. Batiste sees him this afternoon. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Final pathology by telephone within a few days, then the head and neck oncology team review after the PET/CT. Otolaryngology will follow him with that team, with laryngoscopy at each review after treatment. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
